usage: tillandsias-plan [--index <path>] <command>
         commands:
           capabilities              ORDER 569. Print this binary's subcommand capability set,
                                     one token per line on stdout and nothing else. The set is
                                     embedded at COMPILE time, so it describes the sources this
                                     artifact was built from. A binary WITHOUT this subcommand
                                     predates order 569 and its capabilities are unknowable —
                                     that absence is itself the stale-binary signal the forge
                                     wrapper branches on.
           capability-matrix         ORDER 808-7yrd. The FLEET HARDWARE matrix, folded from the
                                     `capabilities:` fragment channel: one row per host_id, its
                                     schedulable (device_class, lane, engine) triples, and its
                                     present-but-unusable devices. Distinct from `capabilities`
                                     above, which reports THIS BINARY's subcommands.
                                     `--by-hardware` (805-r98w) re-keys it on (fingerprint,
                                     substrate) and reports control=yes|no per hardware group,
                                     so a caller can ASK whether a substrate control exists
                                     rather than assume one; unidentified rows are listed, never
                                     bucketed together.
           experts-probe             ORDER 718-ja7g. Which expert TIER is live on this host, as
                                     ONE line: l0 (file-backed, always ready) / l1 (retrieval —
                                     needs BOTH an embeddings endpoint and a built index) / l2
                                     (synthesis). Closed vocabulary per tier: ready | unset |
                                     unreachable | no-index | scheme-unsupported | malformed.
                                     `advice=` names ONE next action. Exit 0 when every
                                     configured tier answers, 1 otherwise — ADVISORY, not a
                                     gate: a dev host with no endpoint is a supported state and
                                     L0 still answers with citations. Both MCP servers consume
                                     this instead of each re-deriving reachability.
           corpus-coverage           Which repository file types the spec/answer corpus indexes,
                                     which it DECLINES and why. Read-only. The declined list is
                                     explicit so an answer's absence can be attributed to a
                                     deliberate boundary rather than mistaken for a retrieval
                                     miss — the two look identical from the asking side.
           check [--strict-fragments]
                                     integrity + schema validation (exit 1 on violations).
                                     ORDER 796-4ydb. A fragment the fold could not parse is
                                     always named on stdout as `malformed: <path>` and the
                                     verdict says `incomplete:` instead of `ok:` — the checks
                                     that passed were run over less than the plan. It exits 0
                                     anyway, because build.sh runs this on every host and a
                                     fleet-wide refusal makes one host's typo every host's red
                                     build (699-dycj). --strict-fragments arms that refusal for
                                     a caller that cannot tolerate a partial corpus: exit 3,
                                     distinct from 1 so 'incomplete' and 'unsound' never blur.
           closure-evidence-check <fragment.yaml>
                                     exit 1 if the fragment sets a closure rung
                                     (completed/verified/done) with no evidence event (686-7qcm)
           declared-closures [--] | declared-closures-check <fragment.yaml>
                                     ORDER 885-92iu. A `verifiable_closure` that NAMES a
                                     litmus test must name one that exists AND that some
                                     spec binds — execution is binding-driven, so an unbound
                                     test runs in no suite and is as inert as a missing one.
                                     Bare form REPORTS over the folded ledger (exit 0, there
                                     is standing debt); `-check` GATES one fragment (exit 1),
                                     so new debt is refused when it is FILED.
           fragment-misplaced-definitions <fragment.yaml>
                                     ORDER 812-d45t. Print the packet_ids of `events:`
                                     entries that carry DEFINITION fields but no `event:` —
                                     a packet written under the wrong key, which every gate
                                     accepts and the fold then drops entirely.
           fragment-event-packets <fragment.yaml>
                                     ORDER 797-qm4t. Print EVERY packet_id an events block
                                     addresses, whatever the event type, one per line, exit 0.
                                     The terminal-only sibling below cannot see a `note` or
                                     `progress` event aimed at a packet_id that does not exist,
                                     so such work is discarded in silence. Same YAML parse and
                                     the same exit 3 on an unparseable fragment.
           fragment-terminal-events <fragment.yaml>
                                     ORDER 752-pst5. Print the packet_ids whose events block
                                     DECLARES a terminal `completed` event (inline packet
                                     `events:` or the top-level `events:` form), one per
                                     line, exit 0. A YAML parse bounds attribution to the
                                     events block, so PROSE that quotes the marker inside a
                                     block scalar is never read as a declaration. Backs the
                                     closure-event pass of check-fragment-status-loss.sh.
           carry-forward-check-batch <fragment.yaml>...
                                     ORDER 1500-gu5r. carry-forward-check over MANY
                                     fragments in one process: one tab-separated line per
                                     result — `<path>\tparsed`, `<path>\tgap\t<id>`,
                                     `<path>\tunreadable\t2`, `<path>\tunparseable\t3` —
                                     in argument order, exit 0. The per-file arm below is
                                     unchanged; a caller probes for this name and falls back.
           carry-forward-check <fragment.yaml>
                                     ORDER 831-ezea. Print the packet_ids this fragment
                                     TOUCHED (has an event for) and LEFT OPEN (no terminal
                                     event, status: value, or declared status) while naming
                                     no `next_action` — one per line, exit 0. Closures are
                                     EXEMPT: a carry-forward note on a terminal row is a dead
                                     letter no selector reads again. Same YAML parse and the
                                     same exit 3 on an unparseable fragment as its three
                                     fragment-* siblings. ADVISORY today (adoption 4.6%),
                                     see scripts/check-carry-forward.sh for the promotion bar.
           arrival-routing-check <fragment.yaml>...
                                     ORDER 831-ezea, the ARRIVAL half of carry-forward.
                                     Print `population|<n>` (rows the named fragments NEWLY
                                     DEFINE, by origin, so a G-Set re-declaration is not an
                                     arrival) then one `overlap|<id>|<predicate>|<sentence>`
                                     per collision with an OPEN row. Predicates: owned_files
                                     (the rule as written, from the same fold answer_next
                                     uses for claim exclusion, at OpenRows scope), and
                                     deliverable / title (normalized string EQUALITY only —
                                     the re-filing class, never semantic sameness). Does NOT
                                     decide the pickup_role disjunct: both roles are printed
                                     for the reader. Exit 0 with overlaps (ADVISORY, bar in
                                     scripts/check-arrival-routing.sh), 3 on a named fragment
                                     the fold could not read.
           next-order [prefix]       mint a COLLISION-FREE order token for a new packet
           validator-surface-hash    what this binary was BUILT from; --current what the
                                     checkout holds now; --check compares them (0 same,
                                     3 different, 4 cannot ask) — 1287-h6qn
           build-id                  which SOURCES this binary was compiled from (984-i4k2)
           source-revision [dir]     what a rebuild from a checkout would bake (984-i4k2)
                                     (<seq>-<suffix>, e.g. 581-k3f9). Never compute the
                                     'next free order' yourself: that reads a ledger snapshot
                                     which is stale the moment another host commits, so
                                     concurrent filers pick the SAME number. The minted token
                                     is PERMANENT — never renumber it. A prefix shared by two
                                     packets is normal. See methodology/distributed-work.yaml
                                     -> order_id_allocation.
           plan-events <packet_id|order>
                                     ORDER 882-vqe4. Print a packet's FOLDED events —
                                     base PLUS fragment overlay — one per line as
                                     <type>\t<ts>. Exists because callers were counting
                                     activity by grepping plan/index.d/ alone, which goes
                                     blind the moment compaction folds a fragment into
                                     the base. Exit 1 only for an unresolvable id; an
                                     existing packet with no events prints nothing and
                                     exits 0, which is what a stranded claim looks like.
           expire-claims [--ttl-hours N] [--write] [--dry-run] [--list-live] [--now-epoch S] [--host H]
                         (default DRY-RUN: writing requires --write; 1067-24q6)
                                     ORDER 672-bz7u. Return stranded in_progress claims to
                                     ready: any packet whose LAST recorded event activity is
                                     older than the TTL (default 24h) gets a status fragment
                                     flipping it back to ready with a progress event naming
                                     the expiry. --dry-run lists without writing. A packet
                                     with NO parseable activity timestamp is reported as
                                     unknown-age and NEVER expired (fail conservative).
           status <id|order>         one packet's status line
           next-action <id|order>    the EFFECTIVE next_action — newest of the
                                     packet field and any event carrying one. Reading
                                     the field alone returns a stale queue, because
                                     `packets:` is a G-Set and cycles append events
                                     rather than re-declaring the packet (864-r9wt).
           blocked-by <id|order>     packets directly blocked by X
           dependencies-of <id|order> X's direct unsatisfied depends_on prerequisites
           blocked-closure <id|order> everything transitively downstream of X
           parked-blocks [id|order]  dependents invisibly blocked behind a PARKED
                                     packet (implemented/needs_clarification/blocked/
                                     failed); no arg = whole ledger (order 686-7qcm)
           split-parents             ORDER 750-zrt4. Packets SPLIT INTO children that
                                     are still `ready`, so the selector keeps offering
                                     them: 722-ecne was ranked urgent= on four
                                     consecutive cycles and claimed by nobody, and
                                     606-bvnp ranked #1 claimable with all four slices
                                     already spoken for. One TSV row per parent, NAMING
                                     the children and their statuses so a host reading
                                     the exclusion knows where the work went. A parent
                                     that legitimately keeps direct work of its own
                                     declares `retains_direct_work: true` and is never
                                     reported; an unresolvable child is skipped, so a
                                     typo cannot make a parent unclaimable. REPORTS
                                     ONLY (exit 0) — auto-obsoleting a split parent
                                     would satisfy its dependents and turn a selection
                                     nuisance into a false green.
           ready [role]              ready packets (optionally for a pickup role)
           next [role] [--release V] [--limit N]
                                     ORDER 606-xu52. The cold-start selector: at most FIVE
                                     cited, release-aware, role-compatible, dependency-clear,
                                     unleased claimable packets, ranked deterministically
                                     (priority, release-targeted first, order). Release
                                     defaults from the folded '## ACTIVE RELEASE' heading;
                                     no release anywhere is a typed refusal. Milestones and
                                     criteria holders are never offered as claims. Natural
                                     aliases via `answer`: "what's next?" and
                                     "what v0.5 work can I do on linux?".
           blocking-counts [--release V] [--limit N]
                                     ORDER 632-retq. `<packet_id>\t<count>` — how many READY
                                     packets each id blocks, counted over EVERY ready packet
                                     rather than one role's, because a packet in another column
                                     is frequently the thing this one waits on.
           forgotten [--limit N] [--min-age-days N] [--now-epoch S]
                                     ORDER 718-jqt5. READY packets the fleet has stopped
                                     noticing, as TSV: order, packet_id, age_days, leased,
                                     blocking. Eventless first (oldest ORDER first, since
                                     next-order mints monotonically), then stalest, then
                                     fewest dependents — a leaf is what a residual-maximising
                                     selector never reaches. Milestones excluded: they are
                                     containers, not claimable work. Total order, no seed:
                                     the same ledger yields the same list on every host.
           select-rows [--claimable-by R] [--release V] [--limit N]
                                     ORDER 632-retq. The batch selector's projection, as TSV:
                                     rank, release_target, order, packet_id, urgency, release.
                                     Already filtered to ready + claimable + release +
                                     dependency-clear + unleased, so the caller needs no jq.
                                     scripts/select-work-batch.sh needed NINETEEN jq calls to
                                     build this, which is why it could not run on a host without
                                     jq; satisfying that dependency per host repeats the yq/ruby
                                     exposure, so the projection moves to the binary that already
                                     owns the ledger.
           query [--status S] [--role R] [--release V] [--tag T]... [--limit N] [--json]
                                     ORDER 582-26mm. THE generic filtered reader over the
                                     FOLDED ledger (base ⊕ plan/index.d/ fragments): the only
                                     correct way to enumerate packets by status / pickup_role /
                                     desired_release / capability_tags. Release matching is exact;
                                     an unknown release is an error, never an ignored constraint.
                                     project-info plan_query and drain-queue
                                     both route here; a reader that forgets fragments reports a
                                     stale ledger with total confidence. TSV by default
                                     (order<TAB>packet_id<TAB>desired_release<TAB>tags); --json
                                     emits the plan_query projection array.
           burndown <milestone>      release-target children with statuses
           answer <question...>      the CITED answer envelope as JSON (order 394b)
           decompose <query...>      adversarial query decomposition via LLM
                                     (order 920-pxg6). Returns a JSON array of
                                     {prompt, kind} variants for concurrent dispatch.
           pipeline <query...> [--domain D]
                                     ORDER 920-pxg6. THE grounded pipeline, CLI front-end:
                                     tier -> decompose -> per-variant domain-filtered
                                     retrieval from the published spec index -> synthesis
                                     over retrieved context -> only-if-used citations ->
                                     Rust validation -> collect merge. Emits the ratified
                                     answer envelope: cited, or the typed unsupported:
                                     refusal — never raw model prose. Same function as
                                     expert-serve (one pipeline, two front-ends).
           expert-serve [--port N] [--root D]
                                     ORDER 920-pxg6. OpenAI-compatible loopback front-end
                                     over the SAME grounded pipeline: 127.0.0.1:11436 by
                                     default, POST /v1/chat/completions (non-stream and
                                     stream:true SSE ending 'data: [DONE]'; the model id
                                     IS the domain: all|spec|code|methodology|cheatsheet),
                                     GET /v1/models, 404 JSON otherwise. Refusals are
                                     rendered VERBATIM as completion content (HTTP 200,
                                     finish_reason stop). Serves until stdin EOF —
                                     </dev/null exits immediately. One pinned stderr line
                                     on start; nothing on stdout.
           collect                   first-wins dedup of responses via Lua runtime
                                     (order 920-pxg6). Reads JSON array from stdin,
                                     returns collected envelope on stdout.
           validate-yaml <file>...   ORDER 746-htj9. Load each file with serde_yaml and
                                     report ok:yaml-loads / blocked:yaml-load-failed /
                                     blocked:yaml-unreadable. THE sanctioned YAML reader:
                                     no interpreter (yq, ruby) exists in all three
                                     environments the gates run in; this binary does.
           yaml-get <file> <path>    ORDER 746-htj9. Read a dotted path and print the
                                     sequence space-joined. Missing key is EMPTY, not an
                                     error; unparseable stays its own verdict (720-24u6).
           yaml-json <file>          ORDER 746-htj9. Emit the document as JSON on stdout,
                                     so rich queries compose with jq — the one query tool
                                     present in ALL THREE environments: yaml-json f | jq '…'.
                                     The yq filters this retires are already jq syntax.
           yaml-type <file>          ORDER 746-htj9. Top-level node kind in yq spelling
                                     (!!map, !!seq, !!str, …), so pre-push can assert
                                     fragment shape without yq.
           verify-answer [--root D]  read an envelope on stdin; exit 1 if any citation
                                     does not resolve or its span does not contain the claim.
                                     ORDER 801-g9nn: also derives caller-relation
                                     (same|behind|ahead|diverged|unfetched|unknown) between D's
                                     HEAD and the answer's commit, and exits 3 — not 1 — when a
                                     citation is SOUND at its own commit but stale here.
           methodology [--root D] [--file S] <yaml.path>
                                     ORDER 394c. YAML path query over methodology.yaml and
                                     methodology/**/*.yaml. Prints the 394b envelope: the
                                     matched block plus a resolvable file:line. An unknown
                                     path is confidence=unsupported, never a guess.
           methodology-ask [--root D] [--file S] <question...>
                                     route a canonical discipline question to its YAML path,
                                     then answer it. Unrouted questions are unsupported.
           methodology-index [--root D]
                                     every indexed path with its file:line (the query surface)
           session-tokens [--since <utc>] [--transcript <path>]
                                     ORDER 1437-3pj7. This session's BILLED tokens from the harness
                                     transcript (message.usage, deduplicated by message.id), plus the
                                     sub-agent totals; source=absent or absent:schema-drift:<field>
                                     with zeros when it cannot measure. Never a guess.
           discipline show [--json] | target --platform <p> | check-ref <ref> | derive [--json]  [--root D] [--seed F]
                                     ORDER 1446-664f: derive observes the project (origin HEAD, integration
                                     branches, work refs, author emails, PR merges, hooks) and prints
                                     derived=<n> seed=<n|none> effective=<n>; an enforced rule refuses only
                                     when its qualifier is observed, else warns :seed-ahead-of-reality.
                                     Observations read the checkout's refs as of its LAST FETCH:
                                     `git fetch origin` first (a note: line says when nothing was fetched).
                                     ORDER 1443-w79y. The branch-discipline seed
                                     (.tillandsias/branch-discipline.yaml): level, per-rule
                                     enforcement, integration branch per platform, ref grammar.
                                     No seed = level 0 advised: nothing is refused. check-ref exits
                                     1 only when an ENFORCED rule refuses the ref.
           policy eval [--host-kind bare-metal|forge|ci] [--regime R] [--caller C] [--cwd D]
                       [--root D] [--seed F] -- <argv...>  |  policy show [--root D] [--seed F]
                                     ORDER 1443-isrk. The command policy: allow | deny | consent for
                                     one argv, from a compiled-in floor (no-shell-strings,
                                     no-credential-mutation, soft-/hard-reset, workspace-destroy,
                                     force-push) plus .tillandsias/command-policies.yaml, which can
                                     only tighten it. Token on stdout, why:/remedy: on stderr;
                                     exit 0 allow, 1 deny, 4 consent.
           msg whoami|send|recv|list|status|lint|gc [--lane <lane>]
                                     ORDER 1506-nvqt. The fleet message bus's LOCAL store: send
                                     (body on stdin or --body-file, never argv) checks the 600-byte/
                                     8-line shape, refuses secret-shaped bodies and out-of-bounds
                                     --ttl (60..604800, default 86400), resolves --to lists and
                                     @groups from plan/fleet/, and prints ok:msg:queued:<id> at once;
                                     status reads the receipt (pending | acked:<mailbox>@<ts> +
                                     via:<rung> | undelivered:<reason>). There is no ack verb: the
                                     ack is the infrastructure's. `msg` alone prints the grammar.
           metrics-log-path <basename> [repo-root]
                                     ORDER 1125-92xa. Where THIS BINARY would put a metrics log:
                                     <checkout>/.cache/metrics/<basename>, falling back to /tmp when
                                     there is no writable checkout. Mirrors scripts/metrics-log-path.sh,
                                     which a Rust binary cannot source — this subcommand exists so the
                                     two implementations can be COMPARED by a gate (arm 1b of
                                     scripts/test-metrics-log-path-agreement.sh) instead of drifting.
                                     Before it, the CLI wrote usage records to a hardcoded /tmp while
                                     every shell reader looked in .cache/metrics: 901 records written,
                                     0 readable. Reads no ledger, so it answers on a broken one.
           append-event <id|order> <type> (--summary-file <path>|-) --ts <ISO> [--agent A] [--host H]
                                     Prose comes from a FILE or stdin so the shell cannot expand it (971-7muc).
                                     A literal <summary> argument still works but is REFUSED when it carries
                                     shell-expansion residue; a balanced substitution is invisible by then.
                                     append an event, VALIDATED before flush (refuses a broken ledger).
                                     --agent defaults from TILLANDSIAS_AGENT_ID and REFUSES when both
                                     are absent; --host defaults to the compiled platform (772-4se9).
                                     WRITE TARGET differs from set-field's, invisibly at the call
                                     site: a NEW plan/index.d/ fragment when the packet was DECLARED
                                     in one, the folded plan/index.yaml otherwise — both print ok:.
                                     Stage plan/ wholesale (git add plan/) BEFORE the gate, or the
                                     event stays local while the ok: line reads as landed (746-htj9;
                                     measured twice on 2026-08-29, on two hosts).
           grade [--root D] [--case ID] [--envelope F|-] [--list-engines] [SET.yaml ...]
                                     ORDER 394d. Grade the experts against the COMMITTED ground
                                     truth. Defaults to openspec/litmus-tests/groundtruth/
                                     expert-groundtruth-rung1.yaml; pass more query sets (or a
                                     glob) to grade further corpora with the same harness.
                                     --envelope grades ONE case against an envelope captured
                                     elsewhere (e.g. off the MCP server). Exit 1 = graded RED,
                                     exit 2 = the harness could not run at all.
           spec-index --out <dir> [--root D]
                                     ORDER 547. Chunk the whole-spec corpus into <dir>/chunks.jsonl
           spec-retrieve --index-dir <dir> --query-vec <f> [--k N]
                                     network-free cosine top-k over caller-supplied embeddings
           score-checks
                                     ORDER 977-j6qu. THE centicolon scorer. Reads
                                     `<check-id> <weight> <pass|fail>` lines on stdin and
                                     prints {earned,denominator,residual,regime} — one
                                     ranking function over the obligation lattice, so
                                     local-ci.sh stops accumulating a second score under
                                     the same name. `regime` names when the score left the
                                     monotone band (a tombstone changes the denominator),
                                     because a bare number cannot say which side it is on.
                                     A malformed line REFUSES rather than scoring a partial
                                     denominator, which would raise the percentage.
           spec-floor --chunks-json <f>
                                     ORDER 821-73es. Apply the grounded pipeline's retrieval
                                     floors (TILLANDSIAS_RETRIEVE_REFUSAL_FLOOR on the best
                                     score, TILLANDSIAS_RETRIEVE_MIN_SCORE per chunk) to a
                                     spec-retrieve result: the kept array, or a typed
                                     out-of-coverage refusal object with the reason ready
           spec-envelope --chunks-json <f> [--answer-file F] [--root D]
                         [--corpus-commit SHA]
                                     build a VERIFIED envelope keeping only the citations the
                                     answer actually used. --corpus-commit stamps the frame the
                                     INDEX was built at onto every citation (order 801-g9nn)
           fragments                 report the append-only plan/index.d/ overlay: which
                                     fragments are live, which are malformed, and whether
                                     compaction is eligible
           compact                   fold every fragment into the base ledger and delete
                                     exactly the ones folded (refuses a lossy rewrite)
           loop-status [--index plan/loop_status.md]
                                     ORDER 582-nqw5. THE loop_status reader: the folded view
                                     (base ⊕ loop_status.d/ fragments). The only correct way to
                                     read loop_status — a reader that forgets fragments reports
                                     a stale status with total confidence.
           loop-status-fragments      ORDER 582-nqw5. Report the loop_status.d/ overlay: live
                                     fragments, malformed ones, and whether compaction is eligible
           loop-status-verify [--ledger P] [--render]
                                     ORDER 626-zmhz. The deterministic ACTIVE-RELEASE + count gate:
                                     cross-checks the FOLDED loop_status prose (exactly one ACTIVE
                                     RELEASE heading, exactly one `— ACTIVE` bullet, and that bullet
                                     IS the active release) against the FOLDED ledger's release
                                     counts, and fails loud on stale prose. --ledger overrides
                                     plan/index.yaml; --render prints the canonical count line.
           set-field <id|order> <field> <value> [--ts ISO] [--host H] [--reason TEXT]
                                     ORDER 636-9m79. Correct a packet field by writing the LWW
                                     channel as a NEW fragment. USE THIS instead of hand-authoring:
                                     re-declaring a packet under `packets:` is a G-Set no-op that
                                     parses, validates and silently does nothing — three hosts hit
                                     that on 2026-08-09 and 11 of 21 completions were discarded.
                                     Field-generic despite the channel's `status:` name (642-fedr).
                                     Resolves against the FOLDED ledger, so unlike append-event it
                                     reaches fragment-only packets (600-c266). Refuses an unknown
                                     reference; reports a no-op instead of writing one.
           loop-status-append [--ts ISO] [--host H] [--suffix S] [--file F]
                                     ORDER 582-nqw5. Append ONE `## Cycle …` section (stdin or
                                     --file) as a NEW fragment file — concurrent hosts each write
                                     their own path, so appending status no longer conflicts.
                                     Refuses `## Direction` and every other non-cycle heading.
           loop-status-compact        ORDER 582-nqw5. Fold fragments into loop_status and delete
                                     exactly the ones folded, gated on: nothing dropped, nothing
                                     lost, operator-owned sections byte-identical, fold idempotent
           loop-status-fragments      ORDER 582-nqw5. Report the loop_status.d/ overlay: live
                                     fragments, malformed ones, and whether compaction is eligible
           lua [--class cacheable|observing | --unsandboxed] <script.lua | -e code> [args...]
                                     1375-btuf: SANDBOXED by default (observing env + json/yaml/hash/path/time);
                                     --unsandboxed is the named raw-VM opt-in (archive-plan-packets.sh only).
                                     Run a Lua script or snippet with the embedded, tillandsias-managed
                                     Lua 5.4 runtime (eliminates heterogeneous external script dependencies).
           predicate <script.lua> [arg] [--class cacheable|observing] [--name fn_name]
                                     Default class: cacheable (no shell, no clock); observing is opt-in.
                                     ORDER 1252-hsrz. Evaluate a Lua predicate with the capability-bounded
                                     predicate runtime (pure shims fs.read/expect.*, shell only in observing class).
                                     Enables forge agents to validate uncommitted specs without host recompilation.

